  "slug": "quantum-error-correction-4176",
  "summary": "Experiments and simulators now resolve correlated noise, leakage and loss in QEC, tying characterized noise to code geometry, circuits and mitigation.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread treats non-ideal hardware noise in error correction and mitigation as structured and measurable rather than idealized Pauli noise. A 105-qubit superconducting experiment scales surface-code stability tests to 7x7 patches and identifies temporally correlated heating-to-|3> leakage. Foliated surface codes are shown to be optimal against space- and time-like skip-gate leakage, and Ellis provides fast Pauli-frame simulation under qubit loss and leakage. Logical error prediction under biased circuit-level noise guides rectangular surface-code geometry selection, cutting qubit overhead. Magic state cultivation with lattice-surgery escapes is demonstrated on IBM hardware. Near-term work extends bnZNE mitigation to randomized circuits and proposes a self-baseline testing oracle with a controlled false-positive rate. The common novelty is linking characterized noise to code design, resource choices and validation.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-24"
}
